WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.800
MARTIN OMANDER: I love
building APIs on Cloud Run,

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:05.260
because it gives me
autoscaling and I only

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:07.780
pay when my code is
handling a request.

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:10.340
But sometimes I
write full web apps,

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:13.930
which contain both a server
API and client assets,

00:00:13.930 --> 00:00:15.550
like HTML files.

00:00:15.550 --> 00:00:17.500
Firebase hosting
is a great option

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:21.910
for serving those static assets,
as it includes a global CDN.

00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:24.100
But you can also
use Cloud Run itself

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:26.590
if you don't mind
going without a CDN.

00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:29.560
Simply tell your web application
framework in your code

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:31.360
that a particular
directory contains

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:34.930
static files to be served
to the client if requested.

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:36.650
Why would you do this?

00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:38.710
Well, it simplifies
coding, as you

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:41.710
don't have to deal with
CORS when JavaScript and API

00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:44.320
calls are in the same domain.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:47.710
It simplifies deployments, as
the full web app is deployed

00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:50.350
within a single container.

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:53.050
I use this approach for
company internal web apps,

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:55.540
where ease of coding
and ease of deployment

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:58.570
are more important than
global scalability.

00:00:58.570 --> 00:01:00.450
Happy coding.

